---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
    <div id="model-body">
        <h1 id="model-title">YOLO Model Fine Tuning</h1>
        <div id="model-text-container">
            <p class="model-body-text">
                I run a Frigate instance (see <a
                    href="/projects/homelab/index.html"
                    target="_blank"
                    class="model-body-link">here</a
                >) which uses ONNX runtime and supports YOLO models. I had been
                using a plain YOLO(NAS) model trained on public data which did
                fine for human detection but often choked at detecting my dog at
                odd angles in my apartment. So I finally had the idea to try to
                fine tune the model I had or a newer one to improve performance,
                I did not realize the rabbit hole I was stepping into.
            </p>
            <p class="model-body-text">
                For a basic understanding (one I can not yet give) read <a
                    href="https://docs.ultralytics.com/"
                    target="_blank"
                    class="model-body-link">here</a
                > on how YOLO works. There are many parameters you can change to get
                an intended outcome but I focused on my size (of the taken pixel grouping)
                and ensuring I had odd angles off a dog in my training data. That
                lead me to another problem . . . training data, thankfully frigate
                keeps clips from motion or audio detections making quality captures
                easy from my home running off of the base model. This however does
                not annotate and allow me to change the data which then led me to&nbsp;
                <a
                    href="https://www.cvat.ai/"
                    class="model-body-link"
                    target="_blank">CVAT</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p class="model-body-text">
                <a
                    href="https://www.cvat.ai/"
                    class="model-body-link"
                    target="_blank">CVAT</a
                > is a very cool open source project that offers the user a, <cite
                    >"labeling suite for teams building real world AI"</cite
                >. To start I made a basic python script to download a YOLO
                model and run it through the images I have to get a rough
                annotation for all (4000+) images.
            </p>
            <p class="model-body-text">
                After running an automated python script to annotate the given
                images the pain staking process of going through images and
                deleting bad ones, touching up bad marks from the past model, or
                improving marks the model didn't make takes enourmous amounts of
                time. Most of which is a continous ongoing process when I have
                free time or am bored enough to improve the training data
                quality.
            </p>
            <p class="model-body-text">
                After training data was annotated enough for me to want to test
                the results against a normal model from <a
                    href="https://www.ultralytics.com/"
                    target="_blank"
                    class="model-body-link">Ultralytics</a
                > after fine tuning one hence the training began. I used simple python
                scripts to rougly divide up training data into categories removing
                a high ratio of backgrounds. Then used <a
                    href="https://www.ultralytics.com/"
                    target="_blank"
                    class="model-body-link">Ultralytics</a
                >&nbsp;
                <a
                    href="https://pypi.org/project/ultralytics/"
                    target="_blank"
                    class="model-body-link">PyPi package</a
                > to fine tune the model against my training data. On my workstation
                with a 10GB 3080 it took rougly 30-60 minutes at 640 image size running
                for a base of 100 epochs. While I am still working on comparing the
                old model with the current one I can say that it does make a difference,
                a model I had tested I had overmarked the person object so the model
                would mistake things like a trash can as a person.
            </p>
            <p class="model-body-text">
                I am roughly on version 3 of the model changing the epochs from
                100 to 200 and the patience (when it stops training i.e. "good
                enough") to 20 the next step is to up the image size to
                hopefully increase the quality the model receives being able to
                detect things with higher accuracy even at lower quality. Check
                back in for updates to this project!
            </p>
            <a
                class="model-body-link"
                href="https://github.com/TeejMcSteez/dataset"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">View source on GitHub &rarr;</a
            >
        </div>
        <a id="back" href="/">Back</a>
    </div>
</Layout>

<style>
    p a {
        white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .model-body-link {
        color: #3b82f6; /* text-blue-500 - same as Lab.astro*/
        text-decoration: none;
    }

    .model-body-link:hover {
        text-decoration: underline;
    }

    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        .model-body-link {
            color: #93c5fd; /* dark:text-blue-300 - same as Lab.astro*/
        }
    }
    #model-body {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
    }
    #model-title {
        text-align: center;
    }
    #model-text-container {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        max-width: 65ch;
        padding: 1rem;
        margin: 1rem;
    }
    #back {
        display: inline-block;
        background-color: #3b82f6; /* bg-blue-500 */
        color: #ffffff; /* text-white */
        font-weight: 700; /* font-bold */
        padding-top: 0.5rem; /* py-2 */
        padding-bottom: 0.5rem; /* py-2 */
        padding-left: 1rem; /* px-4 */
        padding-right: 1rem; /* px-4 */
        border-radius: 0.25rem; /* rounded */
        transition: all 0.3s ease-in-out; /* transition duration-300 ease-in-out */
        margin-top: 2rem; /* mt-8 */
        margin-bottom: 1rem; /* mb-4 */
        text-decoration: none;
    }
    #back:hover {
        background-color: #1d4ed8;
    }
</style>
